INT. JURY ROOM, OLD GRAIN EXCHANGE - DAY

A long table of scarred oak. Tall windows with blinds half drawn. A pitcher of water sweats. Dust motes spin.

Six jurors sit in a row. FOREWOMAN GERTRUDE OYELOWO, sixty, taps a pen. A YOUNG MAN IN A HOODIE slouches. MR. CORNELIUS BELLWETHER, seventy, in a bow tie, polishes his glasses. A WOMAN WITH KNITTING, plump, needles clicking. A BUS DRIVER in uniform, arms crossed. And a QUIET MAN at the end, thin, bald, who has said nothing.

GERTRUDE
We'll go around. Verdict?

BELLWETHER
Guilty.

THE YOUNG MAN
Not guilty.

KNITTING WOMAN
Guilty.

BUS DRIVER
Guilty. Obviously.

GERTRUDE
Juror six?

The quiet man looks up. He sets a hand flat on the table.

QUIET MAN
Not guilty.

BELLWETHER
On what grounds?

QUIET MAN
Reasonable doubt.

BUS DRIVER
There's no doubt.

QUIET MAN
There's some.

KNITTING WOMAN
(counting stitches)
Where?

QUIET MAN
The light switch.

GERTRUDE
What about it?

QUIET MAN
The defendant said he flipped it on. But the switch was installed upside down. Up means off.

Silence. The needles stop clicking. Bellwether puts his glasses back on.

BELLWETHER
Remarkable.

THE YOUNG MAN
Told you.

GERTRUDE
What's your name, Juror six?

ANSELM
Anselm Rudd. Electrician.

BUS DRIVER
(muttering)
Of course.

ANSELM
I'll explain it again, if anyone wants.

GERTRUDE
We have all day.
